The posture you saw at 08:17—head and neck pressed down or forward near the floor while the pelvis and tail lift high into the air, as if he is doing a "stoppie" on a motorbike—is a classic avian neurological sign known as ventroflexion of the neck with compensatory pelvic elevation (or a downward-pitched gait).


Because you are tracking this empirically for Branquinho, separating the mechanical observation from the possible physiological causes helps clarify what his nervous system is actually doing during those episodes.


1. The Biomechanics of the "Stoppie" Posture


Birds rely heavily on their head and neck—along with their vestibular (inner ear) system and spinal proprioception (sensors in their joints and muscles that tell the brain where the body is in space)—to maintain their center of gravity.




Loss of Proprioceptive Feedback: When inflammation, nerve damage, or a nutritional deficiency disrupts signals between the legs/pelvis and the cerebellum (the brain's balance center), the bird loses an accurate sense of where its feet are relative to its body.


Visual & Tactile Anchoring: By lowering his head and neck toward the floor, Branquinho is using his neck as a counterweight and bringing his eyes and beak closer to the ground to stabilize his visual horizon.


Compensatory Pitch: Shifting his center of gravity sharply forward onto his neck and chest causes the lighter rear end (pelvis and tail) to tip upward. Moving forward in this pitched-down stance allows him to keep walking without toppling backward.




2. Primary Neurological Triggers in Roosters


Because this behavior happens occasionally (intermittently) rather than constantly, it points toward a fluctuating trigger rather than a fixed structural injury:




Thiamine (Vitamin B1) or Vitamin E / Selenium Deficiency: Even if he is receiving B12, an imbalance or depletion in B1 (thiamine) or Vitamin E affects the cerebellum and peripheral nerves. B1 deficiency classically causes sudden, intermittent neck flexion ("star-gazing" in reverse, where the head curls downward toward the chest/floor) that worsens under mild stress or excitement—such as anticipating the coop opening at 08:17.


Vestibular or Cerebellar Inflammation: Mild, lingering inflammation in the inner ear or brain (from a past viral exposure, low-grade infection, or toxin) can cause episodic vertigo. When a wave of dizziness hits, pitching forward is a reflexive attempt to stop the room from spinning.


Cervical or Thoracic Nerve Compression: Pressure or minor inflammation along the spinal cord (such as from an old physical strain, sparring impact, or roosting awkwardness) can intermittently pinch motor or sensory pathways to the hind limbs, causing him to pitch forward to relieve tension or regain footing.


Pre-Opening Excitement / Blood Pressure Shift: Notice the timing—08:17, right before coop opening. Anticipation increases heart rate and excitement. In birds with an underlying neurological vulnerability, sudden excitement or rapid head movements can briefly overload their balance processing, triggering a short episode that resolves once they settle down.




3. Practical Logging Markers to Watch


Since you are already separating raw observations from hypotheses, adding two quick notes whenever this posture appears will help narrow down the trigger:




Duration & Recovery: Does he snap out of it within seconds once the coop door opens and his focus shifts, or does it linger for several minutes? (Quick resolution leans toward excitement-triggered vestibular overload or mild nerve pinch; prolonged episodes lean toward metabolic/vitamin depletion.)


Head Tilt or Pupil Focus: During the episode, is his head straight down, or is one ear tilted slightly toward the floor? A slight rotational tilt strongly points to the inner ear (vestibular system) rather than the spine.




