1. Why Doubling Supplements Won't Fix This (and Carries Risks)




NVV (Natura Veta Vit): You are right to be cautious about Vitamin E. At 22,000 IU per kg, NVV is a concentrated multivitamin containing fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, and E). Unlike B vitamins, fat-soluble vitamins accumulate in the liver and body fat rather than flushing out in droppings. Increasing NVV by 50% or 100% over weeks risks hypervitaminosis (especially Vitamin D and A toxicity), which causes kidney strain, calcium deposits in soft tissues, and paradoxically worsens weakness and neurological symptoms. Keep NVV at its standard baseline dose.


BIO+B12: Water-soluble B vitamins are safe in modest short-term boosts because excess flushes out, however, many Thai commercial "Bio + B12" poultry powders also include antibiotics (such as oxytetracycline or sulfas) or electrolytes alongside B12. Check the label carefully: if it contains an antibiotic, doubling the dose harms gut flora and stresses the kidneys, and extra B12 will not repair physical scar tissue in the neck.




2. How the Chickhood Neck Injury Explains the "Stoppie"


Knowing that Tóly ripped the skin off Branquinho's neck as a small chick—and that Bico Preto suffered the exact same attack twice—completely reframes what is happening:




Cervical Scar Tissue & Proprioceptors: A chicken's neck is packed with dense clusters of proprioceptive nerve endings (sensors in the muscles, tendons, and fascia that tell the brain where the head and body are in space) as well as superficial cutaneous nerves. When neck skin and underlying fascia are torn off a tiny chick, healing leaves dense, less-elastic scar tissue and altered nerve pathways.


Sudden Stretch / Pinch Trigger (Lasting a Few Seconds): When Branquinho is inside the movable coop waiting for it to open, he is alert, pacing, and turning or extending his neck sharply. A specific angle or sudden stretch can momentarily tug on that old cervical scar tissue or briefly irritate a superficial neck nerve.


The Reflexive Reset: For 2 to 5 seconds, his brain receives a scrambled "falling backward / off-balance" signal from his neck sensors. He instinctively drops his head toward the floor to anchor his center of gravity (raising his rear), and as soon as his neck angle shifts back and the nerve signal clears, he walks normally again.




